From 4f0c277ce5d14b588e0410d37285f15a25c64414 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(entities): preserve Bedrock spatial updates

Bedrock move-entity-delta packets may omit unchanged axes. Merge only the
supplied coordinates for local corrections and remote actors so omitted
axes cannot become zero. Preserve the rule that ignores non-forced local
updates.

Protocol 2193 also sends MotionPredictionHints instead of ordinary actor
velocity updates when spatial optimizations are enabled. Translate its
runtime ID and motion through the same Java velocity writer, retain its
ground state, and discard updates for actors that no longer exist. The
Mojang 1.26.51 metadata archive establishes the field order and omission
of the tick carried by SetActorMotion. Registered-handler tests verify
the encoded Java packet, unsigned runtime IDs, ground transitions, and
removed actors without replacing their positions.

Keep the last received movement packet position and rotation separate
from the mutable actor transform. Native delta handler 14133ef30 merges
omitted fields from the cached absolute payload, and publisher 14103f580
writes that cache before its ignored-local-transform guard. Live
prediction or other actor writes must not become received packet data.

Retain both absolute and sparse updates before local admission. Preserve
the initial actor placement as the bootstrap, then retain all six spatial
fields exclusively through these movement packets. A removed actor has
no cache update; a replacement actor owns separate state.

The matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 executable/protocol2193 passes 512 fresh
handler/publisher controls over all six omission bits, ground states,
distinct live positions and removed actors. Registered Java handler tests
compare the native outputs and verify independent live state and encoded
remote positions/rotations. These cases do not establish complete actor
construction, interpolation ticking, all lifetimes or full trajectories.

Seed received angles with the native spawn bootstrap's wrapped byte
conversion. Native helper 14103ff60 normalizes each float angle, divides
by 360 and multiplies by 256 before truncating to a signed byte. Retaining
the original spawn floats changes omitted angles in the first sparse
packet. Keep the live spawn rotation independent from this packed cache.

Original inline proxy initializers, spawn bootstrap and sparse handlers
pass 1024 additional controls, including wrap boundaries and large finite
angles. Actual registered Java handlers match their position and angle
outputs. Both deliberate regressions, full-float retention and unwrapped
byte conversion, fail the mandatory and native-reference comparisons.
Surrounding spawn flow and allocation, complete interpolation phases and
native trajectories remain separate verification requirements.

Retain movement flags and every unsigned 64-bit interpolation-duration
bit with the received packet cache, including updates ignored by local
transform admission. Native delta handler 14133ef30 creates an absolute
payload with high flag bits set, explicit ground/teleport/local-force/
force-completion bits and the complete duration. Absolute packets replace
that metadata with their wire flags and native zero duration. The spawn
bootstrap preserves constructor flags and duration. Do not narrow the
counter source before native target construction consumes it.

Another 192 original handler/publisher controls cover force completion,
ground state, removed actors and unsigned durations through UINT64_MAX.
Registered Java handlers match the received metadata and spatial fields.
Both duration truncation and dropped force completion fail the mandatory
regression and native comparison. Full interpolation target application,
normal phases, actor ownership and native trajectories remain unfinished.
